\id HOS Original Douay-Rheims (1582/1610)
\usfm 3.0
\ide UTF-8
\h Osee
\toc1 The Prophecy of Osee.
\toc2 Osee
\toc3 HOS
\mt1 The Prophecy of Osee.
\is THE PROPHECIE OF OSEE.
\ip \it OSEE\it* borne \it in Belomoth\it* (as writeth S. Epiphanius) \it of the tribe of Iſſachar\it*, prophecied \it in the\it* \f - \ft Oſee of the tribe of Iſſachar prophecieth the captiuitie of both Kingdoms.\f* \f - \ft \+it De vita & interitu prophetar. 4. Reg. 17. S. Iero. Ep. ad Paulin.\+it*\f* \it reigne of Ozias\it* (otherwiſe called Azarias) \it Ioathan, Achaz, Ezechias\it*, Kinges of Iuda, and of \it Ieroboam\it* the ſonne of Ioas King of Iſrael; and of the reſidue of the Kinges of Iſrael, euen to their captiuitie, which happened in the ſixth yeare of Ezechias King of Iuda. This Prophet \it taking\it* by Gods commandment \it a fornicatrixe to wife, and hauing children that became alſo fornicators\it*, by theſe figures, and by \it a widow long expecting another huſband\it*, and the like parables, and other preaching, admonisheth both the Kingdomes of Iſrael and Iuda, that for their obſtinacie in ſinnes, they shal fal, firſt the one and afterwardes the other, into miſerable captiuitie. Exhorteth them to repentance; \f - \ft Their relaxation.\f* foresheweth their releaſe; and the coming of Chriſt our Redemer, with aboundance of grace, \f - \ft And coming of Chriſt. (b)\f* and benefites to al nations.
\is THE ARGUMENT OF THE TWELVE LESS PROPHECIES.
\ip \it WHY Iſai, Jeremie, Ezechiel\it*, and \it Daniel\it* are called the Foure greater Prophets, & theſe twelue \f - \ft Four are called the greater Prophets, and twelve the leſs.\f* \f - \ft \+it S. Iero. Prologo. li. Reg. S. Aug. li. c. 18. c. 29. civit. Theod. Iſidorus.\+it*\f* the leſs, there ſeemeth no other certain and proper reaſon, but becauſe they writ more largely, and theſe more briefly. For otherwiſe without eſſential difference, all the ſixteen, as alſo Baruch (whoſe Booke is inſerted with Ieremies) and Moyſes, Samuel, the Royal Pſalmiſt David, Nathan, Elias, Elizeus, Eſdras, Nehemias, & many others, ſome writing Books, ſome not, were abſolutely true Prophets of God, endued with the holy ſpirit of prophecy; had the like revelations, with \f - \ft All theſe and many others were as properly Prophets as the chiefeſt.\f* the ſame aſſurance of truth, in great part of the ſame Myſteries, as wel pertaining to the old Teſtament, as to the New. And ſo theſe twelue, \it contracted into the ſtraitneſs of one volume\it* (ſaith S. Jerome.) multo aliud, quam ſonant in litera, praefigurant: \it Prefigurate a farre other thing, than they\it* \f - \ft \+it Epiſt. ad Paulin.\+it*\f* \it ſound in the letter\it*. Signifying, as he elſewhere explicateth, that they do foreshew manie \f - \ft \+it In Oſee. 1.\+it*\f* important things, not only pertaining to the Jews, and ſome other peoples of thoſe former times, but alſo of all nations to be converted to Chriſt. They were not all at one time: but \it Oſee, Ioel,\it* \f - \ft Of theſe 12, ſix propheſied before the captivity of the 10. tribes.\f* \it Amos, Abdias, Jonas\it*, & \it Micheas\it*, propheſied before the captivity of the ten Tribes. \it Nahum,\it* \f - \ft Other three alſo before the captivity of the two tribes, & three after the relaxation.\f* \it Habacuc\it*, & \it Sophonias\it*, after that captivity, & before the captivity of the two Tribes. And the other three \it Aggaeus, Zacharias\it*, and \it Malachie\it*, after the relaxation from captivity. Neither did they all propheſy in the ſame places: nor concerning the ſame people; and ſo have their particular arguments, as we shall briefly note of every one, as they follow in order.
\ip Here we may note for inſtruction of the vulgar reader, that the Prophets commonly vſe one of theſe names, when they direct their ſpeech of the \f - \ft Special names ſignifying the Kingdom of two tribes.\f*
\ip Becauſe \it Juda\it* was the chiefe, and moſt worthy tribe. \it Benjamin\it* the other only tribe (beſides Levi) that ioyned with Juda. \it Jeruſalem\it* the Metropolitan & Royal citie, where both the Temple, and King's palace were ſituated. \it The Houſe of David\it* is the familie, whereof ſucceeded all the Kings of that Kingdom, ſo long as it ſtood; and of which ſome remayned in more eſtimation than any other euen to Chriſt.
\ip Likewiſe they uſe ſome of theſe other names, when they ſpeak of the \f - \ft Others ſignifying the ten tribes.\f*
\ip For that their firſt King Jeroboam was of the tribe of \it Ephraim\it*, and ſo deſcended from \it Joſeph; Samaria\it*, and \it Jezrahel\it* were the chiefeſt cities of that Kingdom; \it Bethel\it* was one of the places (Dan the other) where Jeroboam ſet up the two calues. VVhich place was otherwiſe, and more truly called \it Bethaven\it*, the houſe of the idol, or of vanitie, or iniquitie. The names alſo of \it Iſrael\it* and \it Jacob\it*, \f - \ft Iſrael & Jacob ambiguouſly ſignify both Kingdoms.\f* were more commonly uſed for the tenne tribes; who being more in number, uſurped and appropriated to themſelves the names of their general Progenitor and Patriarch. Yet ſometimes theſe names importe all the tvvelue tribes, including alſo Levi. And ſometimes, eſpecially after the captivity of the ten tribes, theſe names ſignify the two tribes only: which more imitated Jacob's ſteps and virtues, than the ten.
\c 1
\cd In ſignification of the peoples idolatrie, the prophet marieth a fornicatrix: 4. by his childrens names foresheweth their great, and long captiuitie: 11. And afterwards their redemption together with al nations.
\p
\v 1 THE word of our Lord, that was made to Oſee the ſonne of Beeri, in the dayes of Ozias, Ioathan, Achaz, Ezechias kinges of Iuda, and in the dayes \f a \fr 1.1 \ft This Ieroboam king of Iſrael died 26. yeares before Ozias: wherby appeareth that Oſee prophecied before Iſaie: who begane nerer the end of Ozias reigne. \+it Iſa. 6\+it*.\f* of Ieroboam the ſonne of Ioas king of Iſrael.
\v 2 The beginning to our Lord of ſpeaking in Oſee: and our Lord ſayd to Oſee: Goe, take thee a wife \f b \fr 1.2 \ft Take a woman that hath bene a fornicatrixe :\f* of fornications, and \f 1 \fr 1.2 \ft \+it * beget.\+it*\f* make children \f c \fr 1.2 \ft and begette children, which vvil alſo be fornicators.\f* of fornications: becauſe the land fornicating shal fornicate from the Lord.
\v 3 And he went, and tooke Gomer the daughter of Debelaim: and she conceiued, and bare him a ſonne.
\v 4 And our Lord ſayd to him: Cal his name Iezrahel: becauſe yet a litle while, & I wil viſite the bloud \f d \fr 1.4 \ft The iſſue of Iehu now reigning, ſolicited the 10. tribes called \+it Iezrahel\+it* to idolatrie, which God here ſayth he vvil reuenge.\f* of Iezrahel vpon the houſe of Iehu, and I wil make the kingdom of the houſe of Iſrael to ceaſe.
\v 5 And in that day I wil breake the bow of Iſrael in the valley of Iezrahel.
\v 6 And she conceiued yet agayne, and bare a daughter, & he ſayd to him: Cal her name, Without mercie: becauſe I wil adde no more to haue mercie on the houſe of Iſrael, but with obliuion I wil forget them. \x - \xt 4. Reg. 19.\x*
\v 7 And I wil haue mercie on the houſe of Iuda, and wil ſaue them in the Lord their God: & I wil not ſaue them in bow and ſword, and in battel, and in horſes, & in horſemen.
\v 8 And she weyned her that was, Without mercie. And she conceiued, and bare a ſonne.
\v 9 And he ſayd: Cal his name, Not my people: becauſe you not my people, and I wil not be yours.
\v 10 And the number of the children of Iſrael shal be as the ſand of the ſea, that is without meaſure, and shal not be numbered. And it shal be in place where it shal be ſayd to them: Not my people you: it shal be ſayd to them: \f e \fr 1.10 \ft Amongſt manie ſinners, ſome are the elected children of God, whom he wil cal to grace & repentance.\f* Children of the liuing God.
\v 11 And the children of Iuda, and the children of Iſrael shal be gathered together: and they shal ſet to themſelues, \f f \fr 1.11 \ft Al the faithful haue one head our Sauiour Chriſt.\f* one head, and shal aſcend out of the earth: becauſe great is the day of Iezrahel.
\c 2
\cd He admonisheth the two tribes of their ſinnes, threatning their captiuitie in Babylon. 1. Sheweth the abundance of grace in the Church of Chriſt, and multitude of nations to be conuerted.
\p
\v 1 SAY \f a \fr 2.1 \ft Ye people of Iuda diſdaine not to cal the tenne tribes & the Gentiles your brethren and ſiſters. For God wil make them alſo his people. as appeareth in the Goſpel, more of the tenne tribes then of the other kingdom beleued in Chriſt: and aftervvards more of the Gentiles, then of the Iewes.\f* ye to your bretheren: My people: & to your ſiſter, She that hath obteyned mercie.
\v 2 Iudge your mother, iudge ye: becauſe she not my wife, and I not her husband. Let her take away her fornications from her face, and her adulteries from the middes of her breſts.
\v 3 Leſt perhaps I ſtrippe her naked, and ſet her according to the day of her natiuitie: and I wil lay her as a wildernes, and wil ſet her as a land vnpaſſible, and wil kil her with drought.
\v 4 And \f b \fr 2.4 \ft God wil not vvithdraw his punishments til the ſinners be penitent.\f* I wil not haue mercie on her children: becauſe they are the children of fornications,
\v 5 becauſe their mother hath fornicated, she is confounded that conceiued them: becauſe she ſayd: I wil goe after my louers, that geue me my breads, and my waters, my wool, & my flaxe, mine oile, and my drinke.
\v 6 For this behold I wil hedge thy way with thornes, and I wil hedge it with a wal, and she shal not finde her pathes.
\v 7 And she shal folow her louers, & shal not ouertake them: and she shal ſeeke them, and shal not find, and she shal ſay: I wil goe, and wil returne to my former huſband: becauſe it was wel with me then, more then now.
\v 8 And she did not know that I gaue her corne, and wine, and oile, and multiplied ſiluer vnto her, & gold, which they haue made to Baal.
\v 9 Therfore wil I returne, and wil take my corne in his time, and my wine in his time, and I wil deliuer my wool, and my flaxe, which couered her ignominie.
\v 10 And now I wil reuele her follie in the eyes of her louers: and there shal not a man deliuer her out of my hand:
\v 11 and I wil make al her ioy to ceaſe, her ſolemnitie, her newmoone, her ſabbath, & al her feſtiual times.
\v 12 And I wil deſtroy her vine, & her figtree: of which she ſaid: Theſe are my rewardes, which my louers haue geuen me: and I wil lay her as a forreſt, & the beaſt of the filde shal eate her.
\v 13 And I wil viſite vpon her the dayes of Baalim, to whom she burnt incenſe, & was adorned with her earlet, and with her tablet, and went after her louers, & forgot me, ſayth our Lord.
\v 14 \f c \fr 2.14 \ft Gods mercie preuenteth by his grace offered to ſinners that they may couert to him if they vvil.\f* For this, loe I wil allure her, & wil leade her into the wildernes: & I wil ſpeake to her hart.
\v 15 And I wil geue her dreſſers of vines out of the ſame place, and the vale of Achor to open hope: and she shal ſing there according to the dayes of her youth, and according to the dayes of her aſcending out of the Land of Ægypt.
\v 16 And it shal be in that day, ſaith our Lord: She shal cal me: My husband, and she shal cal me no more, Baalim.
\v 17 And I wil take away the names of Baalim out of her mouth, and ſhe shal no more remember their name.
\v 18 And I wil make with them a league in that day, with the beaſt of the filde, and with the foule of the heauen, and with that, which creepeth on the earth: and bow, and ſword, and battel I wil deſtroy out of the earth: and I wil make them ſleepe confidently.
\v 19 And I wil deſpouſe thee to me for euer: and I wil deſpouſe thee to me in iuſtice, and iudgement, and in mercie, and in commiſerations.
\v 20 And I wil deſpouſe thee to me in faith: and thou shalt know that I am the Lord.
\v 21 And it shal be in that day: I wil heare, ſayth our Lord, I wil heare the heauens, and they shal heare the earth.
\v 22 And the earth shal heare wheate, and wine, and oile: and theſe shal heare Iezrahel.
\v 23 And I wil ſow her vnto me into the earth, and \f d \fr 2.23 \ft The Apoſtles S. Paul. Rom. \+it 9. v. 25\+it*. and S. Peter. \+it ep. 1. c. 2. v. 10\+it*, expound this place of the conuerſion of Gentiles to Chriſt.\f* I wil haue mercie on her, that was Without mercie.
\v 24 And I wil ſay to Not my people: My people art thou: and it shal ſay: Thou art my God.
\c 3
\cd The prophet is commanded againe to loue an aduoutreſſe: 3. whom he maketh long to expect her husband: to ſignifie Gods loue to the Synagogue: 4. and the Iewes ſtate in the new teſtament. 5. who at last shal be conuerted to Chriſt.
\p
\v 1 AND our Lord ſayd to me: Yet againe goe, loue a woman beloued of her frend, and an aduoutreſſe; \f a \fr 3.1 \ft Notwithſtanding ſinners forſake God, yet he offereth them nevv grace, neuer hating the perſons but their ſinnes.\f* as our Lord loueth the children of Iſrael, and they haue reſpect to ſtrange goddes, and loue the kernels of grapes.
\v 2 And I \f 1 \fr 3.2 \ft \+it * bought.\+it*\f* digged her vnto me for \f b \fr 3.2 \ft The Iewes not beleeuing in Chriſt, but expecting his coming, and in the meane time abſtayning from ſeruice of idols, receiue of God temporal poore meanes to liue, not \+it 30. peeces of ſiluer, & three cores of wheat\+it*, that is, beleeuing in the B. Trinitie, & keeping the tenne commandments, they might poſſeſſe life euerlaſting, but halfe ſo much, neither wheate but barley, til nere the end of the world: when they shal be conuerted to Chriſt.\f* fiftene peeces of ſiluer, and for a core of barley, and for halfe a core of barley.
\v 3 And I ſayd to her: Thou shalt expect me manie dayes: thou shalt not fornicate; & thou shalt be no mans: but I alſo wil expect thee.
\v 4 Becauſe manie dayes shal the children of Iſrael ſit without king, & without prince, and without ſacrifice, and without altar, and without ephod, and without \f c \fr 3.4 \ft Theraphim ſignifying images good or bad, being here ioyned with \+it king, prince, ſacrifice, altar\+it*, & \+it ephod\+it*, muſt nedes ſignifie lawful images, ſuch as were religiouſly vſed in the temple of God. \+it 3. Reg. 7. v. 36\+it*.\f* theraphim.
\v 5 And after this the children of Iſrael shal returne, & shal ſeeke the Lord their God, and Dauid their king: and they shal dread at the Lord, and at his goodnes in the laſt dayes.
\c 4
\cd Diuers great ſinnes of both kingdomes, 3. are the cauſe of great punishments threatned, 15. yet the ſinnes of Iuda are leſſe excuſable, becauſe they haue more meanes to ſerue God.
\p
\v 1 HEARE the word of our Lord ye \f a \fr 4.1 \ft Children of Iſrael importe the whole people of the tenne, & two tribes, ſo he ſpeaketh to al til. \+it v. 15\+it*.\f* children of Iſrael, becauſe there is iudgement to our Lord with the inhabitants of the land: for there is no truth, and there is no mercie, and there is \f b \fr 4.1 \ft Knowlege of God includeth the keping of his commandments \+it For he that ſaith; he knoweth God, and kepeth not his commandments, is a lyer. 1. Ioan. 2. v. 4\+it*.\f* no knowlege of God in the land.
\v 2 Curſing, and lying, and manſlaughter, and theft, and aduoutrie haue ouerflowed, and bloud hath touched bloud.
\v 3 For this shal the land moorne, and euerie one shal be weakened that dwelleth in it, in the beaſt of the filde, and in the foule of the heauen: yea and the fishes of the ſea shal be gathered together.
\v 4 But yet let not euerie man iudge: and let not a man be rebuked: for thy people are as thoſe, that gayneſay the prieſt.
\v 5 And thou shalt fal to day, and the prophete alſo shal fal with thee: in the night I made thy mother hold her peace.
\v 6 My people haue held their peace, becauſe they had not knowlege: becauſe thou haſt repelled knowlege, I wil repel thee, \f c \fr 4.6 \ft Function of prieſts which is properly ſacrifice, being taken away, al ſpiritual offices decay therwith.\f* that thou doe not the function of prieſthood vnto me: and thou haſt forgotten the law of thy God, I alſo wil forget thy children.
\v 7 According to the multitude of them ſo haue they ſinned to me: their glorie I wil change into ignominie.
\v 8 They shal eate \f d \fr 4.8 \ft The hoſtes offered for ſinnes.\f* the ſinnes of my people, and at their iniquitie shal lift vp their ſoules.
\v 9 And as the people, ſo shal the prieſt be: & I wil viſite their wayes vpon them, and their cogitations I wil render to them.
\v 10 And they shal eate and shal not be filled: they haue fornicated, and haue not ceaſed: becauſe they haue forſaken our Lord in not obſeruing.
\v 11 Fornication, and wine, and drunkenes take away the hart.
\v 12 My people hath asked in their wood, and their ſtaffe hath declared vnto them: for \f e \fr 4.12 \ft Certaine ſinnes more then others do obſcure mans vnderſtanding, but ſpiritual fornication blindeth the hart aboue al other vices.\f* the ſpirit of fornications hath deceiued them, and they haue fornicated from their God.
\v 13 Vpon the heads of mountaines they did ſacrifice, and vpon litle hilles they burnt incenſe: vnder the oke, and the poplartree, and the terebinth, becauſe the shadow therof was good: therfore shal your daughters fornicate, and your ſpouſes shal be aduoutreſſes.
\v 14 I wil not viſite vpon your daughters when they shal fornicate, and vpon your ſpouſes when they shal commit aduoutrie: becauſe they themſelues conuerſt with harlots, and with the effeminate they did ſacrifice, and the people not vnderſtanding shal be beaten.
\v 15 If thou fornicate \f f \fr 4.15 \ft It was a greater ſinne in the kingdom of Iuda to commit idolatrie, where they had the publique true ſeruice of God in the temple, then in Iſrael vvhere Ieroboam had ſet vp calues, and forbid the people from going to Ieruſalem.\f* fô Iſrael, at the leaſt let not Iuda offend: and enter ye not into Galgal, and goe not vp into Bethauen, neither ſweare ye: Our Lord liueth.
\v 16 Becauſe Iſrael hath declined as a wanton cow: now wil our Lord feede them, as a lambe in latitude.
\v 17 Ephraim is partaker of idols, let him alone.
\v 18 Their banket is ſeparated, with fornication they haue fornicated: the protectours therof loued to bring ignominie.
\v 19 The ſpirit hath bound him in his winges, and they shal be confounded at their ſacrifices.
\c 5
\cd The prophet reprehendeth the prieſtes, and princes of both kingdomes, for drawing the people to idolatrie. 8. denouncing captiuitie for the ſame.
\p
\v 1 HEARE ye this \f a \fr 5.1 \ft There were no true prieſts in the tenne tribes \+it (3. Reg. 12\+it*.) but he calleth them by the title which they vſurped falsly pretending to do the offices of right Prieſtes.\f* ô prieſtes, and attend ye houſe of Iſrael, and you the kinges houſe harken: becauſe there is iudgement for you, becauſe you are become a ſnare to ſpeculation, and a nette ſpred vpon Thabor.
\v 2 And victims you haue declined into the depth: and I the teacher of them al.
\v 3 I know Ephraim, and Iſrael is not hid from me: becauſe now hath Ephraim fornicated, Iſrael is contaminated.
\v 4 They wil not geue their cogitations to returne to their God: becauſe the ſpirit of fornications is in the middes of them, and they haue not knowen the Lord.
\v 5 And the arrogancie of Iſrael shal anſwere in his face: and Iſrael, and Ephraim shal fal in their iniquitie, Iudas alſo shal fal with them.
\v 6 In their flockes, and in their heardes they shal goe to ſeeke the Lord, and shal not finde: he is taken away from them.
\v 7 They haue preuaricated againſt the Lord, becauſe they haue begotten ſtrange children: now shal a moneth deuoure them with their partes.
\v 8 \f b \fr 5.8 \ft The captiuitie is here deſcribed not only in bare wordes but as in fact it shal happen with tumultes of warre ſounding of trumpets, crying, howling &c.\f* Sound with the trumpet in Gabaa, and with the shaulme in Rama: howle ye in Bethauen, behind thy backe ô Beniamin.
\v 9 Ephraim shal be in deſolation in the day of correction: in the tribes of Iſrael I haue shewed faith.
\v 10 The princes of Iuda are become as they that take the bound: I wil power out my wrath as water vpon them.
\v 11 Ephraim is ſuffering calumnie, broken in iudgement: becauſe he began to goe after filthines,
\v 12 And I as it were a mothe to Ephraim: and as the rotte to the houſe of Iuda.
\v 13 And Ephraim ſaw his ſicknes, and Iuda his band: and Ephraim went to Aſſur, and ſent to the king reuenger: and he shal not be able to heale you, neither shal he be able to looſe the band from you.
\v 14 Becauſe I as it were a lioneſſe to Ephraim, and as a lions whelpe to the houſe of Iuda: I, I wil take, and goe: I wil take away, and there is none that can deliuer.
\v 15 Going I wil returne to my place: vntil you fayle and ſeeke my face,
\c 6
\cd By afflictions the people wil returne to God, and hope in Chriſt to come. 4. both the kingdomes ſinning (6. and thincking to be ſpared for their ſacrifices, neglecting workes of mercie) 7. shal be punished. 11. but at laſt deliuered from captiuitie.
\p
\v 1 IN their tribulation early they wil riſe vp to me: Come, and let vs returne to our Lord.
\v 2 Becauſe he \f a \fr 6.2 \ft God vvho puniſheth, is alwayes readie to heale ſinners, if they wil repent.\f* hath wounded, and wil heale vs: he wil ſtrike, and wil cure vs.
\v 3 He wil reuiue vs after two dayes: in \f b \fr 6.3 \ft S. Paul \+it (1. Cor. 15. v. 4\+it*) not only teaching Chriſts Reſurrection, but alſo expresly ſaying \+it the third day, according to the Scriptures\+it*, ſemeth to vnderſtand this place, vvhere & in no other place, the day is ſo cerly expreſſed.\f* the third day he wil raiſe vs vp, and we shal liue in his ſight. We shal know, and we shal folow, that we may know our Lord. As the morning light, is his coming forth prepared, and he wil come to vs as a shower timely, and late to the earth.
\v 4 What shal I do to thee Ephraim? what shal I doe to thee Iuda? your mercie as a morning clowd, and as the dew paſſing away in the morning.
\v 5 For this haue I hewed in prophets, I haue killed them in the wordes of my mouth: and thy iudgements shal come forth as the light.
\v 6 \x - \xt Mat. 9. v. 13.\x* Becauſe I would mercie, & not ſacrifice: and the knowlege of God more then holocauſts.
\v 7 But they as Adam haue trangreſſed the couenant, there haue they preuaricated againſt me.
\v 8 Galaad a citie of them that worke idol, ſupplanted with bloud.
\v 9 And as it were the iawes of men that are robbers, partaker with the prieſts, of them that in the way kil thoſe that paſſe out of Sichem: becauſe they haue wrought wickednes.
\v 10 In the houſe of Iſrael I ſaw an horrible thing: there the fornications of Ephraim: Iſrael is contaminated.
\v 11 Yea and thou Iuda put thee an harueſt, when I shal conuert the captiuitie of my people.
\c 7
\cd Since Ieroboam made ſchiſme in religion that kingdom hath fallen into manie diſtreſſes: 10. and not repenting shal indure more.
\p
\v 1 WHEN \f a \fr 7.1 \ft God punishing al the Ieues by diuiding their kingdom into two \+it (3. Reg. 12\+it*.) would haue cured them: but Ieroboam king of the tenne tribes drevv al his people into groſſer ſinnes then before: by making a ſchiſme & ſetting vp idols to be adored for God.\f* I would heale Iſrael, the iniquitie of Ephraim was reueled, and the malice of Samaria, becauſe they haue wrought lying, and the theefe hath entered in ſpoyling, the robber without.
\v 2 And leſt perhaps they may ſay in their hartes, that I haue remembred al their malice: now haue their owne inuentions compaſſed them, they haue bene done before me.
\v 3 In their malice they haue reioyced the king: and in their lies the princes.
\v 4 Al they committing aduoutrie, as it were an ouen heated of the baker: the citie was quiet a litle from the comiſtion of leauen, til the whole was leuened.
\v 5 The day of our king, \f b \fr 7.5 \ft Al the chief men of the tenne tribes conſented to the ſchiſme & idolatrie of their nevv king Ieroboam.\f* the princes begane to rage by reaſon of wine: he ſtreched out his hand with the ſcorners.
\v 6 Becauſe they haue applied their hart as an ouen, when he lay in wayte for them: he ſlept al the night baking them, in the morning himſelf heated as a fire of flame.
\v 7 Al were heated as an ouen, and haue deuoured their iudges: al their kinges are fallen: there is none amongſt them that crieth vnto me.
\v 8 Ephraim him ſelf was \f c \fr 7.8 \ft Became like to pagane idolaters:\f* mingled in the peoples: Ephraim is become as harth-baken-bread, \f d \fr 7.8 \ft and impenitent.\f* that is not turned.
\v 9 Strangers haue eaten his ſtrength, and he knew not: yea hore heares alſo were powred out on him, and he was ignorant.
\v 10 And the pride of Iſrael \f e \fr 7.10 \ft For al theſe ſinnes they shal be ſeuerely punished.\f* shal be humbled in his face: neither did they returne to the Lord their God, & they haue not ſought him in al theſe.
\v 11 And Ephraim is become as a doue ſeduced, not hauing an hart: they inuocated Ægypt, they went to the Aſſyrians.
\v 12 And when they shal goe forth, I wil ſpred my nette vpon them: as a foule of the heauen wil I plucke them downe, I wil beate them according to the hearing of their aſſemblie.
\v 13 Wo to them, becauſe they haue reuolted from me: they shal be waſted becauſe they haue preuaricated againſt me: & I redemed them and they haue ſpoken lies againſt me.
\v 14 And they haue not cried to me in their hart, but they howled in their chambers: vpon wheate and wine they chewed the cudde, they are reuolted from me.
\v 15 And I haue taught them, and ſtrengthened their armes: and againſt me they haue thought malice.
\v 16 They returned, that they might be without yoke: they became as a deceitful bow: their princes shal fal by the ſword, for the furie of their tongue. This is their ſcorning in the Land of Ægypt.
\c 8
\cd The Chaldees shal deſtroy the temple. 3. But the tenne tribes shal first be caried into captiuitie, 6. for worshiping the image of a calf.
\p
\v 1 IN thy throte let there be a trumpet as an eagle vpon \f a \fr 8.1 \ft The temple alſo in Ieruſalem (though not ſo ſowne) shal be deſtroyed.\f* the houſe of the Lord: for that they haue tranſgreſſed my couenant, and haue preuaricated my law.
\v 2 Me they shal inuocate: My God, we \f b \fr 8.2 \ft But firſt the tenne tribes of Iſrael for their general idolatrie shal be caried into captiuitie by the Aſſyrians \+it v. 9. & 4. Reg. 17\+it*.\f* Iſrael haue knowen thee.
\v 3 Iſrael hath caſt away the good thing, the enemie shal perſecute him.
\v 4 They haue reigned, and not of me: they haue bene princes, and I knew not: their ſiluer, and their gold they made idols to themſelues, that they might perish.
\v 5 Thy calfe is caſt of ô Samaria, my furie is wrath againſt them. How long can they not be clenſed?
\v 6 Becauſe it ſelf alſo is of Iſrael: the workman made it, and it is not God: becauſe the calfe of Samaria shal be as ſpiders webbes.
\v 7 Becauſe they shal ſow winde, and reape a whirlewind: there is no ſtanding ſtalke in it, the bud shal not yeld meale: and if it doe yeld, ſtrangers shal eate it.
\v 8 Iſrael is deuoured: Now is he become in nations as an vncleane veſſel.
\v 9 Becauſe they went vp to Aſſur, a wilde aſſe ſolitarie to himſelf: Ephraim haue geuen gifts to louers.
\v 10 Yea and when they shal hyre the nations with reward, now wil I gather them together: and they shal reſt a while from the burden of the king, and the princes.
\v 11 Becauſe Ephraim hath multiplied altars to ſinne: altars are made to him vnto ſinne.
\v 12 I wil write to him my manifold lawes, which haue bene accounted as ſtrange.
\v 13 They shal offer hoſtes, they shal immolate flesh, and shal eate, and the Lord wil not receiue them: now wil he remember their iniquitie, and wil viſite their ſinnes: they shal returne into Ægypt.
\v 14 And Iſrael hath forgotten his maker, and hath built temples: and \f c \fr 8.14 \ft The two tribes ſeing the miſeries of the other tenne, wil not preuent the like by penance, but thinke to eſcape by fortifying their cities.\f* Iudas hath multiplied fenſed cities: and I wil caſt fire on his cities, and it shal deuoure the houſes therof.
\c 9
\cd The tenne tribes shal ſuffer famine: 12. be depriued of their children: 15. and made captiue.
\p
\v 1 BE not glad Iſrael, reioyce not as the peoples: becauſe thou haſt fornicated from thy God, thou haſt loued reward aboue al the flores of wheate.
\v 2 The flore and the winepreſſe shal not feede them, and the wine shal lie to them.
\v 3 They shal not dwel in the land of our Lord: \f a \fr 9.3 \ft Manie of the kingdom of Iſrael by reaſon of famine and other diſtreſſes, wil flee into Ægypt, as both this place, and the ſame wordes \+it ch. 8. v. 13\+it*. do foreshew. \+it S. Ierom. & Theodoret. v. 6\+it*.\f* Ephraim is returned into Ægypt, and among the Aſſyrians he hath eaten the thing polluted.
\v 4 They shal not offer wine to our Lord, and they shal not pleaſe him: their ſacrfices as the bread of mourners: al that shal eate it, shal be contaminated: becauſe their bread for their ſoule, shal not enter into the houſe of our Lord.
\v 5 what wil you doe in the ſolemne day, in the day of the feſtiuitie of our Lord?
\v 6 For behold they are gone forth from deſtruction: Ægypt shal gather them together, Memphis shal burie them: their ſiluer to be deſyred the nettle shal inherite, the burre in their tabernacles.
\v 7 The dayes of viſitation are come, the dayes are come of retributions: know \f b \fr 9.7 \ft Iſrael did foolishly prophecie al proſperity to themſelues,\f* ô Iſrael the foolish prophete, \f c \fr 9.7 \ft not inſpired by God, but replenished with furie of madnes.\f* the mad ſpiritual man, for the multitude of thine iniquitie, the multitude alſo madnes.
\v 8 The watchman of Ephraim with my God: the prophete is made a ſnare of ruine vpon al his wayes, madnes in the houſe of his God.
\v 9 They haue ſinned deeply, as in the dayes of Gabaa: \x - \xt Iudic. 19. 1. Reg. 8. 4. Reg. 9.\x* he wil remember their iniquitie, and wil viſite their ſinne.
\v 10 As grapes in the deſert I found Iſrael: as the firſt fruites of the figtree in the toppe therof I ſaw their fathers: but they haue entered into Beelphegor, and are alienated into confuſion, and are become abominable, as thoſe thinges, which they loued.
\v 11 Ephraim as a bird hath flowen away, their glorie from birth, and from the wombe, and from conception.
\v 12 But if they shal nourish vp their children, I wil make them without children among men: yea & wo to them, when I shal depart from them.
\v 13 Ephraim, as I ſaw, \f d \fr 9.13 \ft The kingdom of Iſrael was as proud and inſolent as Tyre. \+it Iſai. 13. Ezech. 26. 27. & 28\+it*.\f* was Tyre founded in beautie: and Ephraim shal lead out his children to the murderer.
\v 14 Geue them ô Lord, what wilt thou geue to them? Geue them a wombe without children, and drie breſts.
\v 15 Al their wicked in Galgal, becauſe there I hated them: for the malice of their inentions I wil caſt them forth out of my houſe: I wil not adde to loue them, al their princes reuolters.
\v 16 Ephraim is ſtrooken, their roote is dried vp, they shal yeld no fruite. But and if they shal haue iſſue, I wil kil the beſt beloued things of their wombe.
\v 17 My God wil caſt them away, becauſe they heare him not: and they shal be vagabunds in the nations.
\c 10
\cd After manie benefites, and aduancement, much affliction shal fal vpon the tenne tribes, for their ingratitude towards God.
\p
\v 1 ISRAEL a vine \f a \fr 10.1 \ft By how much more & greater benefites Iſrael receiued of God: ſo much vvere they more vngratful.\f* thicke of branches, the fruite is made equal to it: according to the multitude of his fruite he hath multiplied altars, according to the plentie of his land he hath abunded in idols.
\v 2 Their hart is diuided, now they shal perish: he shal breake their idols, he shal deſtroy their altars.
\v 3 Becauſe they wil now ſay: We haue no king: for we feare not our Lord: and what shal a king do to vs?
\v 4 You ſpeake wordes of vnprofitable viſion, and you shal make a couenant: & iudgement shal ſpring as bitternes vpon the furrowes of the filde.
\v 5 The kine of \f b \fr 10.5 \ft The houſe of one of their calues which Ieroboam ſet vp for their God, otherwiſe called \+it Bethel\+it*.\f* Bethauen haue the inhabitans of Samaria worshipped: Becauſe his people mourned vpon him, & his temple wardens reioyced vpon him in his glorie, becauſe it departed from him.
\v 6 For he alſo was caried vnto Aſſur, a gift to the king Reuenger: confuſion shal take Ephraim, & Iſrael shal be confunded in his owne wil.
\v 7 Samaria hath made her king to paſſe as froth vpon the face of water.
\v 8 And the excelſes of the idol the ſinne of Iſrael shal be deſtroyed: the burre and the thiſtle shal grow vp ouer their altars: and they shal ſay to the mountaines: Couer vs; and to the litle hilles: Fal vpon vs. \x - \xt Iſa. 2. Apoc. 6.\x*
\v 9 From the Dayes \f c \fr 10.9 \ft From the time that the tribe of Dan, adored an idol vvhich they tooke from Micheas \+it (Iud. 18. v. 14\+it*. which the other Iſraelites reuenged not) they haue very often either committed or ſuffered idolatrie, vvhich they ought to haue hindered and therfore shal at laſt be punished.\f* of Gabaa, Iſrael hath ſinned, there they ſtood: the battel in Gabaa vpon the children of iniquitie shal not apprehend them.
\v 10 According to my deſire I wil chaſtiſe them: and the peoples shal be gathered together vpon them, when they shal be chaſtiſed for their two inquities.
\v 11 Ephraim an heifer taught to loue threshing, and I haue paſſed ouer the beautie of her necke: I wil aſcend vpon Ephraim, Iudas shal plough, Iacob shal breake the furrowes to him ſelf. \x - \xt Iere. 4.\x*
\v 12 Sow to your ſelues in iuſtice, and reape in the mouth of mercie, fallow ground: but the time to ſeeke our Lord, when he shal come that shal teach you iuſtice.
\v 13 you haue ploughed impietie, you haue reaped iniquitie, you haue eaten the fruite of lying: becauſe thou haſt truſted in thy wayes, in the multitude of thy ſtrong ones.
\v 14 A tumult shal ariſe in thy people: & al thy munitions shal be deſtroyed as Salmana was deſtroyed by his houſe that tooke vengeance on Baal in the day of battel, the mother being dashed in peeces vpon the children. \x - \xt Iudic. 8.\x*
\v 15 So hath Bethel done to you, becauſe of the malice of your iniquities.
\c 11
\cd The kingdom of Iſrael is further admonished, and threatned, 10. of which tribes manie shal beleue in Chriſt.
\p
\v 1 AS the morning paſſed, hath the king of Iſrael paſſed away. Becauſe Iſrael was a child, and I loued him: and \f a \fr 11.1 \ft Literally this is ſpoken of the people of Iſrael called Gods ſonne \+it (Exo. 4. v. 22\+it*.) vvhom he deliuered out of Ægypt but myſtically is verified of Chriſt called out of Ægypt. \+it Mat. 2\+it*. and is no leſſe certaine the true myſtical ſenſe, the Euangeliſt indued vvith the Holie Ghoſt, ſo interpreting then is the literal ſenſe of this or anie other place. · Mal. 2.\f* out of Ægypt I called my ſonne. \x - \xt Mat. 2.\x*
\v 2 They called them, ſo they departed from their face: they immolated to Baalim, & ſacrificed to idols.
\v 3 And I as it were the nurſe of Ephraim, caried them in myne armes: and they knew not that I cured them.
\v 4 In the cordes \f b \fr 11.4 \ft God draweth men by ſweete inuitations by great & manie benefites agreable to mans nature and freevvil by his loue and charitie: not as beaſtes are dravven by feare & force.\f* of Adam I wil draw them, in the bands of charitie: and I wil be to them as lifting vp the yoke vpon their cheekes: and I declined to him that he might eate.
\v 5 He shal not returne into the Land of Ægypt, and Aſſur he his king: becauſe they would not conuert.
\v 6 The ſword hath begune in his cities, and it shal conſume his elect, and shal eate their heades.
\v 7 And my people shal hang vpon my returne: but a yoke shal be put vpon them together, which shal not be taken away. \x - \xt Gen. 19.\x*
\v 8 how shal I geue thee Ephraim, protect thee Iſrael? how shal I geue thee as Adama, lay thee as Seboim? My hart is turned within, my repentance is diſturbed together.
\v 9 I wil not doe the furie of my wrath: I wil not returne to deſtroy Ephraim: becauſe I am \f c \fr 11.9 \ft Gods propertie is to haue mercie and to pardon.\f* God, and not \f d \fr 11.9 \ft Man is prone to reuenge and punish.\f* man: in the middes of thee the Holie one, and I wil not enter into the citie.
\v 10 They shal walke after our Lord, as a lion wil he roare: becauſe he wil roare, and the children of the ſea shal feare.
\v 11 And they shal flie away as a bird out of Ægypt, and as a doue out of the Land of the Aſſyrians: and I wil place them in their houſes, ſaith our Lord.
\v 12 Ephraim hath compaſſed me in denying, and the houſe of Iſrael in deceite: but Iudas a witneſſe is deſcended with God, and with the ſainctes, faithful.
\c 12
\cd The people by their ſinnes procure their owne miſeries, 3. not regarding Iacobs vertues.
\p
\v 1 EPHRAIM \f a \fr 12.1 \ft To make shevv of turning to God, and to truſt more in men is as vayne as to thinke to fede or to gouerne the vvinde.\f* feedeth the winde, and foloweth the heate: al the day he multiplieth lying and waſte: and he hath made a league with the Aſſyrians, and he caried oyle into Ægypt.
\v 2 The iudgement therfore of our Lord with Iuda, and viſitation vpon Iacob: according to his wayes, and according to his inuentions he wil render to him.
\v 3 \x - \xt Gen. 25. & 32.\x* In the wombe he ſupplanted his brother: and in his ſtrength he was directed with the Angel.
\v 4 And he preuailed againſt the Angel, and was ſtrengthened: and he wept, and beſought him: in Bethel he found him, an there he ſpake with vs.
\v 5 And our Lord the God of hoſtes, the Lord is his memorial.
\v 6 And thou shalt conuert to thy God: keepe mercie and iudgement, and hope in thy God alwayes.
\v 7 Chanaan in his hand a deceitful balance, he hath loued calumnie.
\v 8 And Ephraim ſayd: But yet I am made rich, I haue found an idol to my ſelf: al my labours shal not finde me the iniquitie, which I haue ſinned.
\v 9 And I the Lord thy God out of the Land of Ægypt, I wil yet make thee ſitte in tabernacles, as in the dayes of feſtiuitie.
\v 10 And I haue ſpoken vpon the prophets, and I haue multiplied viſion, and in the hand of the prophets I haue bene reſembled.
\v 11 If an idol in Galaad, then in vaine were they in Galgal immolating with oxen: for their altars alſo as heapes vpon the furrowes of the filde.
\v 12 \f b \fr 12.12 \ft The hiſtorie of Iacob and his children written in Geneſis and Exodus vvhich the prophet here toucheth, sheweth the ſingular benefites of God tovvards this people. · Gen. 28.\f* Iacob fled into the countrie of Syria, \x - \xt Gen. 28. Exo. 14.\x* and Iſrael ſerued for a wife, and for a wife he kept her.
\v 13 But by a prophete our Lord brought Iſrael out of Ægypt: and by a prophete he was preſerued.
\v 14 Ephraim hath prouoked me to wrath in his bitternes, and his bloud shal come vpon him, and his reproch his Lord wil reſtore to him.
\c 13
\cd For their obſtinacie in idolatrie, 7. greateſt plagues are threatned: 10. from which none shal be able to deliuer them. 14. But at laſt Chriſt coming wil redeme al by his death.
\p
\v 1 EPHRAIM \f a \fr 13.1 \ft VVhen Ieroboam firſt ſet vp the calues to be adored the people had horrour therof yet conſented therto.\f* ſpeaking, horrour inuaded Iſrael: and he ſinned \f b \fr 13.1 \ft and shortly after ſome added the idol of Baal. \+it 3 Reg. 16\+it*.\f* in Baal, and died.
\v 2 And now they haue added to ſinne: and they haue made to themſelues \f c \fr 13.2 \ft and of other idols: as this place teſtifieth\f* a molten of their ſiluer as it were the ſimilitude of idols, the whole is the worke of crafteſmen: to theſe they ſay: Immolate men adoring calues.
\v 3 Therfore they shal be as a morning cloude, and as a morning dew paſſing away, as duſt caught with a whirlewind out of the floore, and as ſmoke out of the chimnie.
\v 4 But I the Lord thy God out of the Land of Ægypt: and God beſide me thou shalt not know, and there is no Sauiour beſide me. \x - \xt Iſa. 43.\x*
\v 5 I knew thee in the deſert, in the land of wildernes.
\v 6 According to their paſtures they were filled, and were made ful: they haue lifted vp their hart, and haue forgotten me.
\v 7 And I wil be vnto them as a lioneſſe, as a leopard in the way of the Aſſyrians.
\v 8 I wil meete them as a beare her yong being violently taken away, and I wil breake in ſunder the inner partes of their liuer: and wil conſume them there as a lion, the beaſt of the filde shal teare them.
\v 9 \f d \fr 13.9 \ft Euils that happen are al of mans owne procurement by his ſinnes: vvherof God is no vvay the auctor or cauſe:\f* Perdition is thine ô Iſrael: \f e \fr 13.9 \ft vvho of his part doth al for the helpe of man: for vvhether he punish or pardon, al is to ſaue men: ſo God is onlie cauſe of helpe and of al good but not of euil as it is euel he is in dede the cauſe of punishment, vvhich is called \+it malum pænæ\+it*, the euil of paine. \+it Amos. 3. v. 6\+it*. but this for amendment during this life, and of iuſtice after death.\f* onlie in me thy helpe.
\v 10 Where is thy king? Now eſpecially let him ſaue thee in al thy cities: & thy iudges, of whom thou ſaydſt: Geue me kinges, and princes. \x - \xt 1. Reg. 8.\x*
\v 11 I wil geue thee a king in my furie, and wil take him away in mine indignation.
\v 12 The iniquitie of Ephraim is bound together, his ſinne is hidden.
\v 13 The ſorowes of a woman in trauel shal come to him, he a ſonne not wiſe: for now he shal not ſtand the confraction of the children.
\v 14 \f f \fr 13.14 \ft 1. Cor. 15. · Heb. 2. · This can not be vnderſtood of temporal death, from vvhich God vvil not deliuer men, nor of violent death, from vvhich he vvould not deliuer thoſe that vvere slaine by the Aſſyrians, but neceſſarily of eternal death, from vvhich the iuſt shal be deliuered.\f* Out of the hand of death I wil deliuer them, from death I wil redeme them: I wil be thy death ô death, thy bitte wil I be ô hel, conſolation is hidden from mine eyes. \x - \xt 1. Cor. 15. Heb. 2. Ezec. 19.\x*
\v 15 Becauſe he shal diuide betwen bretheren: our Lord wil bring a burning winde riſing from the deſert: and it shal drie vp his vaines, and shal make his fountaine deſolate, and he shal ſpoyle the treaſure of euerie veſſel that is to be deſired.
\c 14
\cd The prophet forewarning the people of their future afflictions, 2. exhorteth them to repentance, and confeſſion of their ſinnes: 5. foreshewing that God wil geue much grace to the penitent. 10. Al which myſteries only the godlie wiſe shal vnderſtand.
\p
\v 1 LET Samaria \f a \fr 14.1 \ft Such imprecations in holie ſcriptures are ſometimes only predictions. as \+it Pſal. 68. v. 23\+it*. & ſo here is prophecied what shal happen to the Iſraelites in Aſſyria. \+it S. Ierom\+it*. ſometimes are the zelous deſires of Sainctes conformable to Gods iuſtice. as \+it Pſal. 149. v. 6. 7. 8. 9\+it*.\f* perish, becauſe she hath ſtirred vp her God to bitternes: let them perish by the ſword, let their litle ones be dashed, and let the wemen with child be cut in ſunder.
\v 2 Conuert ô Iſrael to our Lord thy God: becauſe thou art fallen in thine iniquitie.
\v 3 \x - \xt Ioel. 2. Zach. 1.\x* Take wordes with you, and conuert to our Lord, and ſay to him: Take away al iniquitie, and receiue good: and we wil render the calues of our lippes.
\v 4 Aſſur shal not ſaue vs, we wil not mount vpon horſe: neither wil we ſay any more: Our goddes the worke of our handes: becauſe thou wilt haue mercie on that pupil, which is in thee.
\v 5 I \f b \fr 14.5 \ft VVhen the Iſraelites ſhal conuert to God, as ſome did vvhen Chriſt came, and manie wil nere the end of the vvorld, then Chriſt wil heale thē.\f* wil heale their confractions, I wil loue them voluntarily: becauſe my furie is auerted from them.
\v 6 I wil be as dew, Iſrael ſhal ſpring as the lillie, and his roote shal breake forth as that of Libanus.
\v 7 His boughes shal goe, and his glorie shal be as the oliuetree: and his ſmel as of Libanus.
\v 8 They shal be conuerted that ſitte vnder his shadow: they shal liue with wheate, and they shal ſpring as a vine: his memorial as the wine of Libanus.
\v 9 Ephraim what haue I to doe any more with idols? I wil heare, and I wil direct him as a verdant firretree: out of me thy fruit is found.
\v 10 \f c \fr 14.10 \ft No humane vvitte is able to vnderſtand this (and other prophetes) yet the iuſt shal know ſo much as is neceſſarie. \+it S. Ierom. in proæm. S. Aug. li. 18. c. 28. ciuit\+it*.\f* Who is wiſe, and shal vnderſtand theſe thinges? of vnderſtanding, and shal know theſe thinges? becauſe the wayes of our Lord be right, and the iuſt shal walke in them: but preuaricatours shal fal in them.
